ZSS wake-on-touch for the NVIDIA 470.256.02 kernel modules (revision 4)

While the driver is suspended through /proc/driver/nvidia/suspend, every
caller waits on a lock until the driver is resumed, in a loop with no timeout.
The driver assumes a whole-system sleep, where callers are frozen; used to
power one GPU off under a running desktop, it leaves X spinning for ever.

With this patch a caller that finds the driver suspended records a wake
request and sleeps. A daemon (zssd) waits on /proc/driver/nvidia/zss_wake,
powers the GPU back on and resumes the driver, and the caller continues.

  /proc/driver/nvidia/zss_wake                    first line: the request count; then
                                                  "waiting N" and the process id of each
                                                  waiting caller, one per line; poll()
                                                  reports a change since the last read
  /sys/module/nvidia/parameters/zss_wake_requests the same count, for diagnostics

The list of waiters lets the daemon keep a GPU off that was switched off on
request, and power it on only for a caller that cannot be left waiting (the
display server).

Revision 4 adds a freeze for a device that vanishes without warning:

  /proc/driver/nvidia/zss_hold    write "freeze": close the gate, stop the
                                  periodic check, mark the devices suspended,
                                  and ask nothing of the hardware
                                  write "thaw": run the ordinary resume, which
                                  initialises a device from nothing, and open
                                  the gate; on failure the driver stays frozen
                                  write "release": open the gate with no resume
                                  reads "frozen" or "running"

  nv_zss_freeze(), nv_zss_thaw(), nv_zss_release() are exported, so that a
  kernel module watching the device can freeze the driver the moment the
  device stops answering, before the driver finds out for itself and gives the
  device up for good. Whether the driver's resume copes without a suspend
  before it is decided inside NVIDIA's closed code.

Nothing changes while the driver is running: the fast path is the same single
trylock as before. Only the open kernel-interface layer is touched.

Licence: the added lines are Copyright (c) 2026 Zerone Laboratories, MIT; the
rest is NVIDIA's. See patches/LICENSE.

Apply with -p1 in the driver source directory, or through DKMS (see
packaging/zss-nvidia-patch).

